#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --quiet --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = ["aiohttp"]
# ///
"""Every wall on every device, on one page, with its controls and its numbers.

    controllers/bitwall-control             # this machine and its tailnet
    controllers/bitwall-control --port 8911 --host 127.0.0.1

A controller in the PROTOCOL.md sense: it talks to receivers, never to
senders. Each device is asked which walls it has, one listing of
`<run>/bitwall/*/door.in` (on Windows, of its pipes: WINDOWS.md, *The
control page on Windows*), and on each wall the page holds one controller
session, `control-<device>-<wall>`, for as long as the page server runs.
The session asks to `follow in`, so the page sees everything the wall
says to anyone and every command it hears, tagged with whose session it
was; `measures <ms>` at the rate chosen gives it the wall's numbers, and
`measures 0` stops them, so a wall nobody is watching the numbers of does
not make them; pictures and mirror frames come on the same session.
Commands go down it with an id, and their answers come back on it. A
session that ends, because the receiver was restarted or the device went
away, is opened again.

Everything the wall says is the untrusted side: wall names, session
names, `sessions` answers and meta are escaped before the page shows
them.

The page is one table of devices, each with its walls in the order they
stand, and nothing on it is sent anywhere but the machine this runs on.
It listens on 127.0.0.1 and on this machine's Tailscale address, and
nowhere else unless told otherwise, because anyone who can reach it can
change every wall: the iPad reaches it over the tailnet, and the house
network does not. Without Tailscale it is this machine alone.
"""
